<!--
  /theming — the @ship/tokens design-system surface. Light/dark via
  data-theme + data-mode, defining a custom theme, and the four token
  categories a SUI app reads.
-->
<div class="doc theming-doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guides</p>
  <h1>Theming</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SUI ships every surface through <code>@ship/tokens</code>: a single CSS
    + TS layer that owns the palette, the surface ramp, the type scale, and
    the motion vocabulary. Every primitive reads from it; nothing in a SUI
    app hardcodes a color, a spacing value, or a radius. Switch the theme on
    the document root and the whole app re-skins in place.
  </p>

  <h2>Light and dark in one attribute</h2>
  <p>
    The shell owns the theme. <code>data-mode="dark"</code> on
    <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> flips the canonical SHIP dark palette; the
    topbar's <strong>Light / Dark</strong> button toggles it. Everything
    theme-driven is keyed off a single CSS custom property — primitives,
    prose, the tutorial editor, the live demos on the home page — so the
    switch is instant and lossless.
  </p>
  <div class="theme-toggle-row">
    <sui-button tone="primary" on:click={() => mode.set('light')}>Light</sui-button>
    <sui-button tone="primary" variant="outline" on:click={() => mode.set('dark')}>Dark</sui-button>
    <span class="muted">active: <code>data-mode="&#123;mode()&#125;"</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code></span>
  </div>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.toggleExample}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Token categories</h2>
  <p>
    Tokens stack in four layers, highest abstraction first. Read the
    highest layer that captures intent; reach for primitives only when
    no semantic token fits.
  </p>
  <ol class="token-ladder">
    <li>
      <strong>Semantic surfaces</strong> — <code>--surface-0..3</code>,
      <code>--backdrop</code>, <code>--card</code>, <code>--popover</code>.
      The card surface is <code>--surface-1</code>; the page ground is
      <code>--surface-0</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tone ramps</strong> — <code>--tone-&#123;name&#125;-&#123;fg|bg|bg-solid|border|ring&#125;</code>.
      Five tones (<code>info</code>, <code>success</code>,
      <code>warning</code>, <code>destructive</code>, <code>accent</code>) plus
      the same shape for <code>primary</code>. Status badges, alerts, callouts
      read the ramp; never compose the base color by hand.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Brand palette</strong> — <code>--primary</code>,
      <code>--accent</code>, <code>--foreground</code>,
      <code>--background</code>, <code>--muted-foreground</code>. The
      five primitives that define a brand voice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Primitives</strong> — <code>--space-&#123;1..8&#125;</code>,
      <code>--radius-&#123;sm,md,lg,xl,full&#125;</code>, <code>--text-&#123;xs,sm,base,lg,xl,2xl,3xl,4xl&#125;</code>,
      <code>--font-&#123;body,display,mono&#125;</code>,
      <code>--ease-&#123;in,out,inOut&#125;</code>. The raw scale the layers above
      compose from.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Define a theme</h2>
  <p>
    A custom theme is a CSS block on <code>:root[data-theme="…"]</code> that
    overrides any subset of the four layers. Register an id via
    <code>data-theme="crimson-red"</code> on the document root and the
    whole app re-skins. The snippet below defines a third theme on top of
    the canonical ship-light defaults.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.defineTheme}></sui-codeblock>
  <p>
    Activate the theme: the host's theme helper (or a direct attribute
    write) sets <code>document.documentElement.dataset.theme = "crimson-red"</code>.
    The shell reads it on the next paint.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.activate}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>The custom theme, live</h2>
  <p>
    The block below uses <code>--primary</code>, <code>--accent</code>, and
    the surface ramp — exactly the tokens a card normally reads. Press the
    button to register the theme; press again to revert.
  </p>
  <div class="theme-card-row">
    <sui-button tone="primary" on:click={toggleCrimson}>
      {crimson() ? 'Revert to SHIP Light' : 'Apply crimson-red theme'}
    </sui-button>
    <span class="muted">active: <code>data-theme="&#123;crimson() ? 'crimson-red' : 'ship-light'&#125;"</code></span>
  </div>
  <sui-card class="custom-theme-card">
    <h3 slot="title">A SUI card, themed</h3>
    <column gap="3">
      <p>The card surface is <code>--surface-1</code>; the heading uses
        <code>--foreground</code>; the body uses
        <code>--muted-foreground</code>. The primary button below uses the
        brand <code>--primary</code>. Nothing hardcoded.</p>
      <row>
        <sui-button tone="primary">Primary action</sui-button>
        <sui-button variant="outline">Secondary</sui-button>
        <sui-badge tone="info">Info badge</sui-badge>
        <sui-badge tone="success">Success badge</sui-badge>
      </row>
    </column>
  </sui-card>

  <h2>Why tokens, not constants</h2>
  <p>
    Hardcoded colors, radii, and spacing values make themes impossible to
    change at runtime and break every app that wants a dark mode, a
    high-contrast variant, or a custom brand. Reading
    <code>var(--primary)</code> instead of <code>#d63b2f</code> lets the
    same component wear any theme the host applies, and the
    <code>color-mix(in oklch, var(--primary) 12%, transparent)</code> pattern
    keeps the same brand voice at every opacity.
  </p>

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/">← Overview</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/styling">Styling →</a>
  </div>
</div>
